  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Multiple rounds of showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected through tonight, with scattered instances of flash flooding \npossible.\n\nA deep southerly flow with a plume of Gulf moisture will arrive \nacross the forecast area today. A wavy cold front will settle \nacross far western New York to the Saint Lawrence River through \nthis afternoon and will be the focus for heavy showers and \nthunderstorms through this evening. Large scale ascent will aid\ndiurnally driven convection within a corridor of high moisture \ncontent and deep (>11kft) warm cloud depths. Southerly winds \nwill be parallel to the front and result in the potential for \nbackbuilding/training as well as repeated rounds of storms.\n\nA low pressure wave is expected to move along the frontal boundary \nacross the forecast area tonight, while remaining ahead of the \nmid level trough axis. The core of the highest PWATS will shift \neast tonight. Heavy showers and thunderstorms are expected \nacross the region with localized areas of heavy rainfall from \nthe Genesee Valley to the eastern Lake Ontario region. \n\nLatest guidance continues to suggest the axis of heaviest rain is \nacross far western New York to the Saint Lawrence River. Basin \naverage rainfall amounts will range from 1-2\" near this axis to 0.5-\n1\" farther east. Localized higher amounts of 3+ inches are possible.\nEfficient rainfall rates and repeated rounds of showers/storms \nfrom the slow moving system could result in localized flash \nflooding through tonight, especially for urban areas and poor \ndrainage areas. A Flood Watch is in effect from 10 AM today \nthrough 2 AM Monday. \n\nConditions will improve from west to east on Monday. The surface low \nis expected to deepen as a shortwave trough drops south from Canada \nMonday. Showers and thunderstorms with heavy rain are possible \nacross the eastern Lake Ontario region through Monday afternoon. \nHigh pressure builds across the region Monday night through Tuesday \nnight resulting in dry weather. \n\nKey Message 2...Unsettled weather moves back into the region \nWednesday through the end of the week. \n\nThe chance for showers and thunderstorms return to the region \nWednesday through Friday. An upper level trough will move into the \nGreat Lakes region and a return flow will bring moisture northward. \nThe synoptic pattern suggests deep southwesterly flow will persist \nduring this time. While confidence is low regarding timing and \nintensity of showers and thunderstorms, chances are higher in the \nafternoon to evening.",
